  - Pair 2 behaves the same way: the 9×9 square becomes 7×7, the 5×5 becomes 3×3, and the 3×3 becomes 1×1.\n- **A single cell (side 1) grows instead.** It becomes a 9×9 block centred on itself.\n  - Pair 3: the lone 5 at (5,9) becomes the block at rows 1–9, columns 5–13, centred on (5,9).\n  - Pair 4: the lone 1 at (4,11) becomes rows 0–8, columns 7–15, again centred.\n  - The size is 9 in both pairs, so I treat it as a constant. Any part of the block that would fall outside the grid is clipped off.\n\n**Order of painting.** I paint the grown blocks first and the shrunken squares on top. In the demonstrations nothing overlaps, so the order does not matter there.\n\n**Applying it to the test input.**\n- The 9×9 square at rows 0–6 extends past the top of the grid, so it is drawn cut off there. Its visible part is 7 rows by 7 columns (rows 0–6, columns 9–15). Removing the ring is applied to the full 9×9 square, which leaves a 7×7 square at rows 0–5, columns 10–14. It is still clipped at the top.\n- Hmm, I should check that properly. The visible bounding box is 7×7 and it is itself a full square, rows 0–6 and columns 9–15. Treating the visible box as the square and taking one ring off gives rows 1–5, columns 10–14.\n- The same question arises in training. Pair 1's 3×3 square of 4s sits at rows 0–2 against the top edge, and it simply shrank to its centre (1,14). That matches \"shrink the visible box by one ring\". The 5×5 square of 1s at rows 11–15 touches the bottom edge and became rows 12–14, which again fits. So the rule is to remove one ring from the visible bounding box, and the test 9s become a 5×5 square at rows 1–5, columns 10–14.\n- The 5×5 square of 4s becomes a 3×3 square at rows 12–14, columns 12–14.\n- Each 3×3 square (the 3s and the 1s) becomes a single centre cell: (1,1) for the 3s and (3,5) for the 1s.\n- The lone 0 at (11,4) grows into a 9×9 block at rows 7–15, columns 0–8.\n\n## Program","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Clear the canvas to background; every square will be redrawn.\")\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    # first: single cells grow into 9x9 blocks centred on themselves\n    for o in objs:\n        if o[\"size\"] == 1:\n            y, x = o[\"cells\"][0]\n            out[max(0, y-4):min(H, y+5), max(0, x-4):min(W, x+5)] = o[\"colour\"]\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Single cell of colour {o['colour']} at ({y},{x}) grows into a 9x9 block centred on it.\")\n    # then: larger squares lose their outer ring\n    for o in objs:\n        if o[\"size\"] > 1:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n            if y1 - y0 >= 2 and x1 - x0 >= 2:\n                out[y0+1:y1, x0+1:x1] = o[\"colour\"]\n            emit(out.copy(), f\"Square of colour {o['colour']} shrinks by one ring.\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Any part of the block that would fall outside the grid is clipped off."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What happens to each square.** Comparing the bounding boxes in the fact sheet for input and output:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The visible bounding box is 7×7 and it is itself a full square, rows 0–6 and columns 9–15."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is to remove one ring from the visible bounding box, and the test 9s become a 5×5 square at rows 1–5, columns 10–14."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Order of painting.** I paint the grown blocks first and the shrunken squares on top."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In the demonstrations nothing overlaps, so the order does not matter there."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c9a50b7a4f4427fdc471f7160e0ae2e3a8156585a4fb288aa19ff4a3533978c6","task_sha256":"bd724d64494b3c82a6ccf430558310d